Text: screens are everywhere in our daily lives. as adults we are constantly switching between working on a computer checking our phones watching tv in the evening sometimes even playing on a tablet at the same time. children are picking up on this behavior with screen time among children increasing. data from the centers for disease control and prevention cdc show that children aged 810 spend on average 6 hours per day in front of a screen. at ages 1114 this increases to nearly 9 hours. 
Answer:

generated_pred:
{
  "Ethical_Stabilizers": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "The text is descriptive and does not cast any actor as a moral guardian, peacemaker, or virtuous authority; it only cites CDC data and describes behavior."
  },
  "Altruistic_Catalysts": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "There is no portrayal of rebels, martyrs, or underdogs driving change or sacrificing for a cause—the passage simply reports screen-use behavior and statistics."
  },
  "Overt_Aggressors": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "No agents are framed as instigators of conflict, violent actors, tyrants, or bigots; the language is neutral and non-confrontational."
  },
  "Deceptive_Subversives": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "The passage contains no insinuation of secrecy, betrayal, espionage, misinformation, or covert manipulation—only observational claims and data."
  },
  "Institutional_Toxins": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "Although the CDC is cited for data, the text does not portray institutions or actors as corrupt or incompetent; the citation is used as an information source."
  },
  "Marginalized_Sufferers": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "Children are described as adopting screen habits and having high screen time, but they are not explicitly framed as victims, exploited, forgotten, or scapegoated—the tone is factual rather than sympathetic or accusatory."
  }
